2026-06-30 15:03:11,676 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-3d111096-7e1c-4b21-bd37-99b816a49aca', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n112017_LysXL_control_F10_KCF.raw\n112017_LysXL_control_F11_14_KCF.raw\n112017_LysXL_control_F6_7_KCF.raw\n112017_LysXL_control_F8_KCF.raw\n112017_LysXL_control_F9_KCF.raw\n112017_vivoXL_control_F10_KCF.raw\n112017_vivoXL_control_F11_14_KCF.raw\n112017_vivoXL_control_F6_7_KCF.raw\n112017_vivoXL_control_F8_KCF.raw\n112017_vivoXL_control_F9_KCF.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nPrediction of an Upper Limit for the Fraction of Inter-Protein Cross-Links in Large-Scale In Vivo Cross-Linking Studies\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nChemical cross-linking and mass spectrometry is of growing use for establishment of distance constraints on protein conformations and interactions. Whereas intra-protein cross-links can arise from proteins in isolation, inter-protein cross-links reflect proximity of two interacting proteins in the sample. Prediction of expected ratios of the number of inter-protein to intra-protein cross-links is hindered by lacking comprehensive knowledge on the interactome network and global occupancy levels for all interacting complex subunits. Here we determine the theoretical number of possible inter- and intra-protein cross-links in available PDB structures of proteins bound in complexes to predict a maximum expected fraction of inter-protein cross-links in large scale in vivo cross-linking studies. We show how the maximum fraction can guide interpretation of reported inter-protein fractions with respect to the extent of sample protein binding, comparing whole cell and lysate cross-linked samples as an example. We also demonstrate how an observation of inter-protein cross-link fractions greater than the maximum value can result from the presence of false positive cross-links which are predominantly inter-protein, their number estimable from the observed surplus fraction of inter-protein cross-links.\nGraphical Abstract\n\n[METHODS]\nMATERIALS AND METHODS\nPDB Structure Files Used in Analysis\nAll 148,586 PDB structure files in mmCIF format were downloaded from the RCSB Protein Data Bank website ftp://ftp.wwpdb.org/pub/pdb/data/structures/divided/pdb/. When multiple PDB files with the same title were encountered, the single one with the highest resolution (lowest Angstrom value) was kept. In addition, if multiple structures contained the same protein constituents and numbers of chains, only a single representative was kept. In each PDB file, all lysine Cα positions and chains were recorded. Each chain in the structure corresponds to a unique bound constituent protein or ligand. All non-identical lysine pairs within specified maximum Euclidean distance were assessed as either inter-protein (originating from different chains in the structure), or intra-protein (originating from the same chain). Protein N-termini were not included as cross-linkable sites since they occur infrequently in cross-linked peptides (comprising less than 0.3% of cross-linked peptides in the XLinkDB database), and are not always included in structure files. Only PDB files having two or more bound constituents, 50 or more lysine pairs, and resolution 5Å or less, were included in the analysis. The number of bound constituents in each PDB structure was determined as its number of chains with potentially cross-linkable lysine residues.\nCalculation of SASD using Jwalk\nJwalk version 1.1 was run locally in a directory with PDB files using the –lys option to specify lysine starting and ending amino acids.\nDetermination of Empirical Cross-Linker Maximum Spans in the Context of Structure Files\nEmpirical cross-link maximum span distances were determined using public datasets available on XLinkDB produced using one of the two different cross-linkers: PIR or DSSO. Intra-protein cross-links originating from a protein for which a PDB structure was available were included. The PIR data comprise 5,028 non-redundant cross-links from all public Bruce Lab datasets, and the DSSO, 1,008 non-redundant cross-links from two publicly available datasets and re-analysis by Mango and XLinkProphet of a third. Distances of each cross-link in the structure were computed as the Euclidean distance between the lysine residue Cα positions attached at each end.\nEffect of FDR on the Observed Fraction of Inter-Protein Cross-Links\nThe following 5 datasets were used in the analysis displayed in Figure 5:\nNuclear DSSO Mango: Data acquired from DSSO treated intact cell nuclei were reanalyzed with Mango, Comet, and XLinkProphet.\nHistone Protein Mixture: A single run acquired from a purified bovine histone protein mixture cross-linked with PIR and analyzed with Mango, as previously described, was analyzed alone with XLinkProphet.\nHeLa PTX: Data from HeLa cells treated with Paclitaxel and cross-linked with PIR (submitted) were acquired with ReACT, searched with a full human proteome database with Comet, and validated with XLinkProphet.\nE.coli Mango: Cells cross-linked in vivo with PIR were analyzed with Mango as described.\nMouse Heart ReACT: Mouse heart tissue cross-linked with PIR and analyzed with ReACT, as described. MS spectra were searched using Comet with a mouse Uniprot database downloaded on April 2018 with 33,936 protein sequences, including decoys.\nIn each case, decoys were included in the XLinkProphet output. When multiple identifications of the same cross-linked peptide pair were present, only that with the lowest (best) maximum Comet expect score was kept. Non-redundant cross-links were sorted by maximum Comet expect score as described in the Results.\nIntact HeLa Cell and Lysate Cross-Linking\nHeLa cells were cultured in RPMI at 37°C and harvested at confluence with 20mM EDTA solution (two 15cm culture dishes per condition, ~4×107 cells). Cell pellets were washed with PBS containing 1mM CaCl2, 1mM MgCl2, and then resuspended in 500μl 170mM disodium phosphate buffer to a 1:1 pellet-buffer ratio by volume. Cells used for intact cross-linking were immediately mixed with BDP-NHP to a final concentration of 10mM, incubated while shaking at room temperature (hereafter RT) for 30 minutes, and then washed with 0.1M ammonium bicarbonate until the pellet was no longer yellow. Cell samples used for lysate cross-linking were first snap frozen in liquid nitrogen and then stored at −80°C until they were cryoground at −80°C with a Retsch MM 400 cryomill for one minute at 30Hz. The milled lysate was allowed to warm to RT and was then cross-linked in the same manner as the intact cells. Samples were mixed with 8M urea to approximately a 1:1 ratio by volume and sonicated using a GE - 130 ultrasonic processor (five pulses at amplitude 40 for five seconds each). Samples were reduced by the addition of TCEP to a final concentration of 5mM and incubated for 30 minutes at RT. Reduced thiols were then alkylated by the addition of iodoacetamide to a final concentration of 10mM and incubated at RT for 30 minutes. The intact cross-linking condition contained 8.09mg of protein, and the lysate condition, 8.23mg, as determined by Bradford assay. Trypsin was added to samples in a 1:200 ratio by protein mass and allowed to digest overnight. Sample digests were centrifuged at 16,000g for 15 minutes. Supernatants were desalted using C18 Sep-Pak cartridges and bound peptides eluted into 50% and 80% acetonitrile (ACN), 0.1% trifluoracetic acid washes (washes were later mixed). Samples were dried using vacuum centrifugation and resuspended in 0.5mL of strong cation exchange solvent A (5mM KH2PO4, pH 2.6, 30% ACN).\nCross-linked peptides were fractionated from samples using strong cation exchange chromatography (Agilent 1200 series HPLC attached to a Phenomenex Luna SCX column). Fractionation was achieved using a 1.5mL/minute flow rate over a 97.5-minute gradient of increasing percentage of strong cation exchange solvent B (5mM KH2PO4, pH 2.6, 30% ACN, 350mM KCl), resulting in 14 fractions total. Fractions were pooled into six larger fractions: 1–5, 6–7, 8, 9, 10, 11–14. Cross-linked peptides were enriched separately from the 5 pooled fractions by the addition of monomeric avidin resin (Ultralink, Pierce) and incubation for 30 minutes at RT. Cross-linked peptides were eluted off the avidin resin with 70% ACN, 1% formic acid, and samples were dried using vacuum centrifugation. Samples were resuspended in 0.1% formic acid and injected into an EASY-nLC 1000 coupled to a Q Exactive Plus mass spectrometer. Samples were separated with a 60cm × 75μm inner diameter fused silica analytical column packed with ReproSil-Pur C8 (5μm diameter, 120Å pore size particles) by applying a linear gradient from 90% solvent A (0.1% formic acid in water), 10% solvent B (0.1% formic acid in acetonitrile) to 60% solvent A, 40% solvent B over 240 min at a flow rate of 300nL/min. The mass spectrometer was operated using a data dependent analysis (DDA) method performing one high-resolution (70,000 resolving power (RP) at m/z 200) MS scan from 400 to 2000m/z followed by MS (17,500 RP) on the 20 most abundant ions with a charge between 4+ and 8+ inclusive detected in the MS. Parameters for MS scans included an automatic gain control target of 50,000 ions, a maximum ion accumulation time of 100ms, an isolation window of 3.0m/z, and a normalized collision energy of 30. A dynamic exclusion window of 30s was used to reduce redundant selection of the same parent ion. MS spectra were processed using Mango 2017.01 rev. 0 beta 3 with mass tolerance relationship set to 40.00ppm, and Mango output was searched using Comet with a human Uniprot database downloaded on April 2018 with 40,632 protein sequences, including decoys. Search results of the 5 fractions were combined separately for the intact (in vivo) and lysate cross-linked samples, and their identified cross-linked peptides validated with XLinkProphet. Non-redundant cross-links were filtered at 1% FDR and uploaded to XLinkDB to assess their inter-protein fractions. The mass spectrometry proteomics data (five raw files and Comet search result pepXML files, each, for HeLa samples cross-linked in vivo and as a lysate) have been deposited to the ProteomeXchange Consortium via the PRIDE partner repository with the dataset identifier PXD013063.\n\n[RESULTS]\nRESULTS AND DISCUSSION\nWe seek to predict the maximal fractions of inter-protein cross-links possible for large-scale samples treated with chemical cross-linkers. Taking advantage of an abundance of high resolution structures of\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
